<div class="main-title">Likutay Halachos – Choshen Mishpat II</div>
<div class="sub-title">Hilchos Matanah u'Matanas Sh'chiv Mayra 4</div>
<div class="sub-title" style="font-size:17px; color:#999; margin-top:-20px;">Laws of Gifts and the Gift of a Dying Person – 4</div>

<!-- HALACHA INTRO -->
<div class="halacha-intro">
Every gift must be revealed and publicized, etc. One must write in the deed of gift: <span class="key-term">"kisvu'hu b'shuka v'chasmu'hu b'vara"</span> [write it in the marketplace and sign it in the open], etc.
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section-num">— 1 —</div>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-indent:0;">
For it is found in the Torah-teaching <span class="key-term">"Achavay lan mana d'la shavya l'chabala"</span> <span class="definition">[Show us a vessel not fit for destruction]</span> <span class="verse-source">[Likutay Moharan I, 25]</span>: that in every world and in every level, there are <span class="key-term">dimyonos</span> <span class="definition">[fantasies/delusions]</span>, etc., which are the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> <span class="definition">[husks/shells of impurity]</span> that precede the fruit and surround the holiness, etc.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And when a person moves from level to level, immediately the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> of that level are aroused and surround him, and he must subdue them and break them, etc. For no two people are the same, etc. And the inner dimension of the lower [level] becomes the outer dimension of the outer [aspect] of the upper [level], etc. And then one must purify and sanctify the place anew, etc.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And it is impossible to subdue the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>, etc., except through the greatness of the Creator, as brought in the <span class="key-term">kavanos</span> <span class="definition">[mystical intentions]</span> of <span class="key-term">Hodu</span> <span class="definition">[the prayer "Give thanks"]</span>. And the revelation of the greatness of the Creator is through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> <span class="definition">[charity]</span> that one gives to a worthy poor person. For the essential greatness and <span class="key-term">p'er</span> <span class="definition">[glory]</span> is the revelation of the <span class="key-term">g'vanim</span> <span class="definition">[colors]</span>. And silver and gold — they are the colors, etc. And they only illuminate when they come to the hand of a Jewish person, etc. And because of this, the nations of the world crave the money of Yisrael, etc. And because of this, [the nations] are called <span class="key-term">rashim</span> <span class="definition">[poor/wicked]</span>, as in: <span class="verse">"Be careful with the <span class="key-term">rashus</span> [authority/poverty],"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
But know: immediately when the non-Jew receives money from a Jew, immediately the <span class="key-term">p'er</span> and the <span class="key-term">chen</span> <span class="definition">[grace]</span> that is within the money become hidden, etc. And this is [the meaning of]: <span class="verse">"They only draw near at the time of their benefit,"</span> etc. See there, all of this well.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
Also know: in order to subdue the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> that surround [one] at every level, one must arouse upon himself the joy of a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>. That is, he should rejoice by remembering that he merited to draw close to Hashem, blessed be He, and he merited to draw close to <span class="key-term">tzadikim</span> who bring him close to Hashem, blessed be He. And through this joy, he breaks the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> and enters the second level. See there.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section-num">— 2 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is known and explained in our words many times: that through all the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> <span class="definition">[commerce/business dealings]</span> that one conducts, one refines holy sparks. And as is explained in the words of our Rebbe, of blessed memory, in the Torah-teaching <span class="verse">"Ashray"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Likutay Moharan I, 35]</span> and in the Torah-teaching <span class="verse">"Ki Taytzay L'milchamah"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Likutay Moharan I, 280]</span> — that through <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> conducted properly, one ascends from level to level, from <span class="key-term">Asiyah</span> to <span class="key-term">Yetzirah</span> <span class="definition">[from the World of Action to the World of Formation]</span>. For <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> conducted properly is in the aspect of the offering of the <span class="key-term">korban tamid</span> <span class="definition">[daily sacrifice]</span> and the <span class="key-term">ketores</span> <span class="definition">[incense]</span>; see there.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And the essential refinement that is accomplished through <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> is completed when the item passes from one domain to another — for then the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> is completed, and then the sparks ascend and are refined, as mentioned above. And because at every level there are the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> that precede the fruit and surround the holiness — therefore one must be very careful to conduct the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> with great holiness, so that the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> that surround there do not take hold of it, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, the transaction is not completed through words alone, but rather through <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> <span class="definition">[formal act of acquisition]</span> — through <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> <span class="definition">[pulling/drawing into one's domain]</span> and <span class="key-term">hagbahah</span> <span class="definition">[lifting]</span>, etc. For one specifically needs the item to enter the domain of the one who acquires it, through <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> and <span class="key-term">hagbahah</span> and the like. For as long as it has not come into his domain, the refinement is not completed. And therefore, even though [the seller] agreed to sell the item and the transaction was verbally concluded — nevertheless, it is still possible that the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> will be aroused and strengthened anew at the time when [the buyer] wishes to bring the item into his domain, and they will ruin the transaction.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For the essential arousal [of the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>] is at the time when the item passes from domain to domain — for then is the essential ascent and refinement, as mentioned above. Therefore, the transaction is not completed through words alone, until one actually performs a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> to bring it into his domain through <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> and <span class="key-term">hagbahah</span>, etc. For as long as it has not entered his domain, [the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>] can be aroused and ruin the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section-num">— 3 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="talmudic-quote">"By Torah law, money effects acquisition, as it says: 'And he shall give the money, and it shall be his' <span class="verse-source">[Leviticus 25:51]</span>. And why did the Sages say that <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> effects acquisition? So that [the seller] should not say to him: 'Your wheat was burned in the upper room.'"</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For the essential revelation of the <span class="key-term">g'vanim</span> <span class="definition">[colors]</span> is in the money of Yisrael, and through the revelation of the colors in the money of Yisrael, the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> at every level are subdued, as mentioned above. And therefore, certainly by Torah law, money effects acquisition — for through money the colors are revealed and the greatness of the Creator is revealed, through which the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> are subdued and the transaction is completed, for [the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>] can no longer ruin it, since they have already been subdued through the revelation of the colors in the money of Yisrael, as mentioned above. And therefore, indeed, by Torah law money effects acquisition, as it is written: <span class="verse">"And he shall give the money, and it shall be his"</span> — <span class="precisely">"and it shall be his"</span> — specifically — for through money there is standing and ascent for the transaction, that is, for the sparks in the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span>. For through money the colors are revealed and the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> are subdued, and then there is standing and ascent for the sparks in the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span>, and through this the transaction is completed by Torah law, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
But why did the Sages say that <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> effects acquisition, etc.? For in truth, even though the money contains the revelation of the colors, this is only for a Jewish person, and even for him the colors only illuminate when he merits to rectify his money through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> to a worthy poor person, as explained there; see there. And therefore the Sages saw that most people who engage in <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> do not merit to rectify their money completely as is proper, and then the colors do not fully illuminate for them.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, even though at the time when [the seller] received the money he agreed to sell the item to his fellow — and this is drawn at its root from the shining of the colors in the money — nevertheless, the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> of the level have still not been subdued, and they can ruin the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span>, because immediately afterward the colors were hidden due to the fact that [the person] has not yet rectified his money completely, as mentioned above. And as our Rebbe, of blessed memory, wrote regarding money that comes into the hands of a non-Jew — that the colors illuminate only momentarily, and afterward their light is hidden, etc., as mentioned above. Likewise, <span class="key-term">l'havdil</span> <span class="definition">[to distinguish]</span>, even for a Jew — even though relative to the non-Jew the colors illuminate for him — nevertheless, since he has not rectified his money as is proper, they do not illuminate for him fully either, but only flash momentarily from within their concealment and are immediately hidden.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> are not subdued through money alone, for the colors do not fully illuminate in it. And therefore the transaction is not completed through money, as mentioned above. For the essential completion of the transaction is according to the subduing of the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> — for then the refinement is complete, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is what they said there: <span class="verse">"And why did the Sages say <span class="key-term">meshichah</span> effects acquisition? So that [the seller] should not say to him: 'Your wheat was burned in the upper room.'"</span> For since he has not rectified his money and the colors were not revealed through it, it can happen that the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> will provoke and take hold of the money even more. For <span class="verse">"this opposite this, G-d has made"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Ecclesiastes 7:14]</span>. And whoever merits to rectify his money through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> — then the colors, which are the greatness of the Creator, are revealed through his money, and he merits to subdue the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> at every level through his money, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
But conversely, when one does not rectify his money through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, then the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> take hold specifically of the money. Because there is a lofty, hidden holiness there — which are the supernal colors that are hidden there — which, when they do not illuminate properly, [the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>] take hold there even more, for they are always drawn after the highest holiness. And from this comes the great strengthening of the desire for money — which is drawn from the fact that the money, at its root, contains very lofty holinesses, which are the supernal colors that are clothed in it. And when one does not merit to reveal the illumination of the colors through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> to a worthy poor person, then the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> take hold of the money even more, and through this one falls into the desire for money. And from this come all the denials and deviations and injustices in business, if one does not overcome his desire for money, G-d forbid.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is what they said — that money does not effect acquisition, so that [the seller] should not say to him "your wheat was burned in the upper room." <span class="precisely">"In the upper room"</span> (<span class="key-term">ba'aliyah</span>) — specifically — that is, at the time when the sparks in the merchandise needed to ascend from level to level, then the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> gained control — which are the destroyers of the world. And they are the aspect of fire, as it is written: <span class="verse">"From the fire they went out"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Ezekiel 15:7]</span>, etc. And through this, the wheat and the merchandise were burned.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="section-num">— 4 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And because of this, one must greatly distance himself from receiving gifts, as our Sages of blessed memory said, and as it is written: <span class="verse">"And one who hates gifts shall live"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Proverbs 15:27]</span>. For the ascent of holiness is difficult to accomplish for free — rather [it must be] with full payment, as is found in the Holy Zohar regarding the acquisition of <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> and good deeds, that one must acquire them with <span class="key-term">agar sh'lim</span> <span class="definition">[full payment]</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> is called <span class="key-term">chinam</span> <span class="definition">[free/gratis]</span>, as is known.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, when one receives an item for free, as a gift, it is very difficult to refine and elevate the holiness that is there. And then the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> that surround every level can take hold of it, and they can destroy his vitality, G-d forbid. For the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> are the <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> <span class="definition">[the side of death]</span>. And therefore: <span class="precisely">"one who hates gifts shall live."</span>
</p>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<div class="section-num">— 5 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore one must write in the deed of gift: <span class="key-term">"kisvu'hu b'shuka v'chasmu'hu b'vara."</span> For the essential ascent is in the aspect of exteriority — for the inner dimension of the lower [level] becomes the outer dimension of the outer [aspect] of the upper [level]. And in particular, the ascent of the sparks in the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> — for <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> in general is in the aspect of outer forms of service, as is understood from the aforementioned Torah-teaching. (Only that within the aspect of exteriority itself, there is certainly inner and outer [dimensions], as is understood from the <span class="key-term">kavanos</span>.)
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, in the gift — because there it is difficult to refine, as mentioned above — therefore the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>, which take hold in the aspect of exteriority, can easily take hold and ruin the gift, and place in the heart of the giver [the desire] to change his word and his will, and ruin the gift. Therefore, the gift is not completed until it is publicized and revealed to all, and [the giver] writes in the deed of gift: <span class="key-term">"kisvu'hu b'shuka v'chasmu'hu b'vara."</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For in the marketplace and outside — there is the essential hold of the <span class="key-term">klippos</span>, which are called for this reason <span class="key-term">chitzonim</span> <span class="definition">[outer ones]</span>. Therefore, one must publicize in the deed of gift: <span class="key-term">"kisvu'hu b'shuka"</span>, etc. — that he commanded to write in the marketplace and outside — for he is giving the gift with a complete heart, in order to nullify the prosecution of the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> and the <span class="key-term">chitzonim</span>, by making the gift known in the marketplace and outside, where their place of hold is, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section-num">— 6 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="talmudic-quote">"One who gives a gift to his fellow must inform him. And they learned this from <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, for the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moshe: 'A good gift I have in My treasure house, and <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is its name, and I wish to give it to Yisrael — go and inform them,' etc."</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For one must inform his fellow beforehand that he wants to give him a gift, so that he will prepare himself to receive the gift. That is, that he will strengthen himself to receive the gift in holiness and purity, for the sake of His Name, blessed be He — so that he will have the power through this to refine the holiness that is in the item of the gift. For because with a gift it is difficult to refine, as mentioned above — therefore one needs preparation beforehand, and therefore one must inform him.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<div class="section-num">— 7 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, which is a good gift that was in His treasure house, which Hashem, blessed be He, informs us of before He gives it to us — so that we will strengthen ourselves to prepare ourselves to receive this good and precious gift. For <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> needs preparation, as our Sages of blessed memory said, as it is written: <span class="verse">"And it shall be on the sixth day, and they shall prepare"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Exodus 16:5]</span>, etc.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, also now that Hashem, blessed be He, has already given us the <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> in His mercy — we need throughout all the days of the week to prepare ourselves to receive this good gift, whose name is <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Remember the day of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> to sanctify it"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Exodus 20:8]</span> — <span class="verse">"remember it from the first [day] of the week"</span> — that if a fine portion comes to hand, he should set it aside for <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>. That is, the aspect of preparation throughout all the six weekdays to receive the good gift that is <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is the aspect of the generality of all the service of a person in this world — which is called <span class="key-term">erev Shabbos</span> <span class="definition">[the eve of Shabbos]</span> — while the World to Come is called <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="talmudic-quote">"Whoever toiled on the eve of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> shall eat on <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>,"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For in truth, it is impossible to serve Hashem, blessed be He, [and thereby deserve reward], as it is written: <span class="verse">"Who has preceded Me that I should repay him?"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Job 41:3]</span> — <span class="verse">"Did anyone make Me a <span class="key-term">mezuzah</span> before I gave him a house?"</span> etc., as our Sages of blessed memory said. And as our master, teacher, and rebbe, of blessed memory, said in the conversation printed in the <span class="key-term">Sipuray Ma'asiyos</span> <span class="definition">[Stories of Ancient Times]</span>; see there.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And all the service of Torah and <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> that He commanded us to do in this world — they are only preparations, for we need to prepare ourselves to receive the gift of His good hand, which He, blessed be He, wants to give us in His mercy in the future. For in truth, all the Torah and <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> are easy and pleasant to do, as it is written: <span class="verse">"Her ways are ways of pleasantness"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Proverbs 3:17]</span>, etc. And by strict law, it would not be fitting to receive reward for them. And all the good reward that He will give is only an undeserved gift, as it is written: <span class="verse">"And Yours, Hashem, is the <span class="key-term">chesed</span>, for You repay a man according to his deeds"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 62:13]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And all the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> are only in the aspect of preparations, to receive through them an undeserved gift from Him, blessed be He — which is the reward of that <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>. For example, by way of illustration: through the fact that we wear <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span>, through this we prepare ourselves to be enwrapped in the light of the <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> and to merit the <span class="key-term">chaluka d'rabbanan</span> <span class="definition">[garment of the Sages / garment of the World to Come]</span>, etc. And likewise, through the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of <span class="key-term">tefillin</span>, we prepare ourselves to be crowned with the <span class="key-term">kisray d'malka</span> <span class="definition">[crowns of the King]</span>, which are the lights of the <span class="key-term">tefillin</span> at their root. For certainly, by strict law, a person does not deserve such wondrous and awesome reward for <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> and <span class="key-term">tefillin</span>, which are easy and pleasant to do. And all the reward he will receive for them is only an undeserved gift.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
Only that Hashem, blessed be He, in His mercy, had compassion upon us and commanded us to do these holy <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> in this world — so that we would have vessels and power to receive His undeserved gift. And likewise with the rest of the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span>. And likewise, regarding all the negative commandments — He cautioned us not to do [certain things] so that we should not come, G-d forbid, soiled to the <span class="key-term">hillula d'malka</span> <span class="definition">[feast of the King]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And it is like a king who warns and informs his beloved ones beforehand that he wants to give them precious gifts and wondrous and exalted treasures, to each and every one, at such and such a time. And out of his love and compassion, he informs them first, so that they will prepare themselves for this — so that they should not come, G-d forbid, soiled with mud and filth to the feast of the king, where the king and his retinue will be. For certainly it would be impossible for them to receive his holy gifts when they are soiled, G-d forbid. They also need to prepare for themselves fine and precious garments, and many types of sweet fragrances — nard and saffron, cane and cinnamon, etc. — and many types of beautiful and fine adornments, so that they will be fit to enter the palace of the king among his great ones and officers and holy and pure servants. And then they will be able to receive the gift of his good hand.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And in his great compassion, he prepared for them beforehand all the items and materials from which they need to make and fashion garments and crowns such as these — so that they would be fit to enter in them the <span class="key-term">haychala d'malka</span> <span class="definition">[palace of the King]</span> — and all types of spices and sweet fragrances with which to perfume themselves beforehand. And he commanded them and cautioned them that throughout the entire time he gave them beforehand to prepare themselves for the feast — throughout all this time, they should occupy themselves with making and fashioning these precious garments and all these holy crowns. And he sent them his faithful emissaries to teach them and inform them how to make and fashion them in every precise detail — so that they should come there in the garments and crowns and adornments that are customary there, so that they should not be ashamed among all the invitees there.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And even more, he commanded them and informed them through his emissaries what to distance themselves from. And he cautioned them very greatly not to contaminate and soil themselves, G-d forbid, with all the abominable things there. And out of his abundant compassion, he also informed them through his faithful ones with what to wash and purify themselves from these contaminations, if they should stumble at times and not be careful of them, G-d forbid. And he established for them many holy springs and many counsels and rectifications to wash and cleanse oneself from all of this. To the point that even one who is very soiled, and immersed in a pit and sunk in miry mud and dressed in soiled garments, G-d forbid — even he can cleanse himself and wash and purify himself in these flowing springs that flow with balsam oil and all types of wondrous fragrances that have the power to transform all types of foul smells into good and precious fragrances.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And he informed them that throughout the entire time he gave them, it is in their power to take from all the items and spices that he prepared for them, and to dress and adorn themselves with them as they wish, and to immerse and purify themselves in all these holy springs that purify and cleanse them from all contaminations — so that they will be able to come clean and pure to the palace of the king, perfumed with myrrh and frankincense, dressed in holy and clean garments that are customary there, crowned with the <span class="key-term">kisray d'malka</span>. And then they will be able to receive all the <span class="key-term">otzros matnas chinam</span> <span class="definition">[treasuries of undeserved gifts]</span> that the king has prepared for them, <span class="verse">"which no eye has seen"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Isaiah 64:3]</span>, etc. — fortunate is one who merits them. And the analogy is understood on its own.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
It turns out that all the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> and good deeds — everything is from Him, blessed be He, from what He has prepared for us in His great mercy. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"For everything is from You, and from Your hand we have given to You"</span> <span class="verse-source">[I Chronicles 29:14]</span>. And it is written: <span class="verse">"Who has preceded Me that I should repay him?"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Job 41:3]</span>. And it would not be fitting to receive reward for them. Only that in His mercy, He wished to give us merit, to give us such <span class="key-term">otzros matnas chinam</span>. Therefore, <span class="verse">"He multiplied Torah and <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> for us"</span> — which are all rectifications and preparations to receive through them the great hidden good, which are the <span class="key-term">otzros matnas chinam</span> that He wishes to give us in His <span class="key-term">chesed</span> and mercy, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="talmudic-quote">"Do not be like servants who serve the master for the sake of receiving a reward; rather," etc.</span> That is, certainly we will receive great good, <span class="verse">"which no eye has seen"</span>, through our good deeds. Only that we must know the truth — that by strict law, we do not deserve a reward, since everything is from Him, as mentioned above. Rather, all our service is not for the sake of receiving a reward, but rather through our service and good deeds we will have the preparation to receive His great <span class="key-term">chesed</span>, which are the <span class="key-term">otzros matnas chinam</span> that He will give to each and every one according to how much he prepared himself in this world for the <span class="key-term">s'udasa d'malka</span> <span class="definition">[feast of the King]</span> and his great ones and intimates. As it is written: <span class="verse">"And to the remnant whom Hashem calls"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Joel 3:5]</span>, etc. And as Rashi explains there.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="talmudic-quote">"This world is like a corridor, and the World to Come is like a banquet hall. Prepare yourself in the corridor so that you will enter the banquet hall."</span> That is, as mentioned above — that all the service in this world is only in the aspect of preparations, in order to enter the palace of the King, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Prepare yourself in the corridor so that you will enter the banquet hall,"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- Interlude before Section 14 - the Rachel/Lavan passage -->

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"And Rachel came with the sheep that were her father's, for she was a shepherdess"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 29:9]</span>. For in truth, the Torah that is deposited in the exile within the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span> <span class="definition">[the imaginative/delusional faculty]</span> — the Torah itself is very holy and pure. And Hashem, blessed be He, has compassion upon His people, and as long as they have not merited to find the point of truth — which is the true leader — then the Torah itself leads Yisrael.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"And Rachel came with the sheep that were her father's, for she was a shepherdess"</span> — <span class="precisely">"she"</span> — specifically — that she herself, who is Rachel, who is the Torah — she herself is the shepherdess who leads the sheep of her father. They are the souls that draw close to the aforementioned leaders who have in them the grasp of Lavan, the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, who do not have the power to lead Yisrael. Rather, Hashem, blessed be He, has compassion upon His people — that the Torah itself, the aspect of Rachel his daughter, leads Yisrael for the time being, until Hashem, blessed be He, arranges circumstances for good.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
[Then] the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Yaakov, sets his eye upon this and employs many stratagems and gives up his life, until he extracts and elevates the Torah — the aspect of Rachel — from the house of Lavan, from the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>. And he unites and bonds with this Torah until he rectifies the entire world through it. For he refines this Torah and elevates it to its root, and through this he innovates wondrous novel insights that are true counsels to lead Yisrael. For only he is able to <span class="verse">"roll the stone from upon the mouth of the well"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 29:10]</span> with ease — <span class="verse">"like one who removes a cap from a bottle"</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is the general [theme of] the entire <span class="key-term">parashah</span> that speaks afterward of how Yaakov took and extracted Rachel and Leah from the house of Lavan, and begat from them all the Twelve Tribes of Y-H. That is, Yaakov — the aspect of the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span> — refined all the Torah, which is called <span class="key-term">aishes chayil</span> <span class="definition">[a woman of valor]</span>, the aspect of Rachel and Leah; he refined everything from the house of Lavan, from the grasp of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> <span class="definition">[imaginative faculty]</span>. And through this he begat the Tribes of Y-H — for the essential existence of Yisrael is through this. And through this he extracted all the sheep from the house of Lavan — which are the holy flocks of sacred sheep — for he extracted them all from the exile of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> and returned them to the truth. For Yaakov is the aspect of the very essence of truth, the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Grant truth to Yaakov"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Micah 7:20]</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 14 -->
<div class="section-num">— 14 —</div>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">akudim, nekudim, u'vrudim</span> <span class="definition">[the bound, spotted, and speckled ones]</span>. They are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">g'vanim</span> — the aspect of the totality of the colors, which are the greatness of the Creator — through which one subdues the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, the bestial power, which are the aspect of the sheep of Lavan. And one refines the good — which are the aspect of the holy sheep, the sheep of Yaakov, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And you are My sheep, the sheep of My pasture, you are man"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Ezekiel 34:31]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And through the <span class="key-term">g'vanim</span>, through which one subdues the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> — through this one merits <span class="key-term">seichel</span> <span class="definition">[intellect]</span> in its completeness, which is: <span class="key-term">seichel b'ko'ach</span> <span class="definition">[intellect in potential]</span>, and <span class="key-term">seichel b'fo'al</span> <span class="definition">[intellect in actuality]</span>, and <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> <span class="definition">[the acquired intellect]</span>. And this is also the aspect of <span class="key-term">akudim, nekudim, u'vrudim</span>, which are the generality of all the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> and the intellectual faculties, which are the generality of all the worlds and their chain of descent from beginning to end — all of which came into being through His wisdom, blessed be He, as it is written: <span class="verse">"You have made them all with wisdom"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 104:24]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For it is found in the <span class="key-term">Eitz Chaim</span> <span class="definition">[the kabbalistic work "Tree of Life"]</span> that the first root of <span class="key-term">Atzilus</span> <span class="definition">[Emanation]</span> and everything that came into being there is called the World of the <span class="key-term">Akudim</span>. And afterward, the lights of the Seven Primordial Kings were drawn forth, which died — for there was death and shattering in them, etc. And this is the aspect of the World of the <span class="key-term">Nekudim</span>. And afterward there was the World of <span class="key-term">Tikun</span> <span class="definition">[Rectification]</span>, through which is the essential coming into being and sustenance of all the worlds from the beginning of <span class="key-term">Atzilus</span> to the end of <span class="key-term">Asiyah</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Vrudim</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And from these three aspects — <span class="key-term">akudim, nekudim, u'vrudim</span> — are drawn and descend the three aspects of the intellect: <span class="key-term">seichel b'ko'ach</span>, etc. For <span class="key-term">seichel b'ko'ach</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Akudim</span>, where there is no apprehension, for it is before <span class="key-term">Atzilus</span>, and from there is drawn the aspect of <span class="key-term">seichel b'ko'ach</span>. And afterward, one brings the intellect from potential to actuality, and then the aforementioned Seven Kings come into being, which are the root of the seven <span class="key-term">midos</span> <span class="definition">[attributes/character traits]</span>, which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">ZA"H</span>. But there, there is still an aspect of shattering — for it is impossible to clarify the intellect to its truth at the very first attempt. And before one gives birth to and innovates something to its truth, one must overturn it and break it initially in many aspects.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And afterward, one refines the fragments and joins them and rectifies them through the addition of new intellect that one draws — and then one builds and completes the innovation in its proper form, as is known. And all of this is understood by whoever merits to innovate novel insights. Or even if one does not innovate on his own, but studies in <span class="key-term">sefarim</span> <span class="definition">[holy books]</span> and understands the ways of the innovation. For usually, one must first propose many reasoning and build many constructs that do not endure — through which one cannot clarify the <span class="key-term">halachah</span> and the innovation to its truth. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"He builds worlds and destroys them"</span> — the aspect of the shattering of the kings, the aspect of the World of the <span class="key-term">Nekudim</span>. All of this occurs at the time when one brings the intellect from potential to actuality, which is the aspect of the building of worlds — for <span class="verse">"You have made them all with wisdom,"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And afterward, even though all the reasonings and intellectual constructs that he initially produced did not endure and were shattered — nevertheless, one must strengthen himself afterward and gather and refine from them many good points, many upright reasonings that are found and scattered among them. And one must add upon them new intellect and join them to it, and gather them together — until the building and the innovation are completed in their truth. And this is the aspect of the World of <span class="key-term">Tikun</span>, the aspect of the World of the <span class="key-term">Vrudim</span>. And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span>, which is the essential establishment of the matter in its proper form. (And see the <span class="key-term">Eitz Chaim</span> and you will understand.)
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And Yaakov employed many stratagems for this in many ways, until he drew the aspect of the revelation of the <span class="key-term">g'vanim</span> upon his flocks of holy sheep — in order to subdue the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> from them and to bring them to the aforementioned intellectual faculties. And this is: <span class="verse">"And Yaakov took rods of fresh poplar, and almond, and plane tree, and peeled white streaks in them"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 30:37]</span>, etc. — <span class="verse">"exposing the white that was on the rods."</span> And it is found [in the Zohar] that this is the aspect of the secret of the <span class="key-term">tefillin</span>, which are the generality of the Torah — for the entire Torah has been compared to <span class="key-term">tefillin</span> — the generality of the <span class="key-term">mochin</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
That is, Yaakov — the aspect of the <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, who is the point of truth — drew with wondrous wisdom all the holy <span class="key-term">mochin</span> in exceedingly wondrous and awesome ways, until he introduced the wisdom and the true intellect into the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> of the totality of Yisrael — so that the wisdom and the true intellect would enter deeply within them, that they would understand the truth to its truest form, so that no deception of the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, the aspect of Lavan, would have the power to divert them from this, G-d forbid.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"And he set the rods that he had peeled in the troughs, in the watering gutters"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 30:38]</span>, etc. <span class="precisely">"In the gutters"</span> (<span class="key-term">b'r'hatim</span>) — these are the <span class="key-term">r'hitay mochin</span> <span class="definition">[pathways/channels of the mind]</span>, as is found in the Holy Zohar. That is, Yaakov set and thrust the rods — which are the aspect of the Torah innovations that he innovated and revealed with such true and wondrous and awesome intellect — he set and thrust them <span class="key-term">b'r'hatim</span>, into the pathways of the minds of his students who draw close to him.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is <span class="precisely">"va'yatzeg"</span> — <span class="precisely">"and he set"</span> — specifically — that he set and thrust his true innovations into the <span class="key-term">r'hatim</span>, into the pathways of their minds, so that they would be thrust deeply within them, to such a great depth that no error of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> would have the power to divert them from them, G-d forbid. For they already understand the truth well.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"<span class="key-term">b'r'hatim</span>, in the watering troughs"</span> — in the pathways of the mind, in the place where they drink the waters of Torah and knowledge — there he set his wondrous rods, which are the aspect of his true intellectual teachings that direct a person at all times toward the straight path — like the rod that directs the flocks toward the straight path. And therefore, leadership and rulership are called by the name "staff" (<span class="key-term">mateh</span>) and "rod" (<span class="key-term">makel</span>), as it is written: <span class="verse">"A strong staff, a rod of rulership"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Ezekiel 19:11]</span>, and similarly in many verses.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"where the sheep came to drink, facing the sheep — and they would become heated when they came to drink"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 30:38]</span> — so that when the other holy sheep would come to drink from the waters of Torah and knowledge, facing the holy sheep that had already drunk, who had already merited to recognize the truth to its truest form — <span class="verse">"and they would become heated when they came to drink"</span> — that they would warm themselves with warmth and fervor of holiness, with fiery sparks — <span class="verse">"a flame of Y-H"</span> — to serve Hashem, blessed be He, and to pray with warmth and fervor. And they would not walk with Him with <span class="key-term">keri</span> <span class="definition">[callousness/coldness]</span>, G-d forbid — in the aspect of coldness that is drawn from the side of Amalek, which is the side of Lavan, of whom it is said: <span class="verse">"who cooled you on the way"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 25:18]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For whoever does not know of the aforementioned point of truth — even one who is an upright man and serves Hashem, blessed be He, and prays and studies Torah — but all his service is with coldness, without any vitality and fervor and warmth of holiness — and Hashem, blessed be He, has no delight from such service, for it is in the aspect of sleep (as explained elsewhere, in the Torah-teaching <span class="verse">"Rabbi Shimon began"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Likutay Moharan I, 60]</span>).
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
But Yaakov — the aspect of the point of truth — drew Torah, the aspect of rods, to lead the world with such wondrous intellectual teachings, until he set and thrust the true intellect into the pathways of the minds of those who draw close to him — so that all the holy sheep that would come to drink from the waters of Torah, facing his holy flock, would warm themselves with warmth of holiness to serve Hashem, blessed be He, and to pray with warmth and fervor of holiness. For the essential warmth of holiness is through the movement of the true intellect (as explained in the Torah-teaching <span class="key-term">"Asika"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Likutay Moharan I, 21]</span>).
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"And it was, whenever the early [strong] sheep would become heated"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 30:41]</span> — and Rashi explains: "the firstborn" — <span class="verse">"Yaakov would set the rods,"</span> etc. <span class="verse">"But when the sheep were feeble, he would not set"</span> them, etc. — and Rashi explains [the word <span class="key-term">ha'atif</span> as] a term meaning "late" / "latter," etc. This is the aspect of what our master, teacher, and rebbe wrote: that the young ones are easier to bring back than the old ones, as is written in the <span class="key-term">Sefer Ha'Alef-Beis</span> <span class="definition">[Book of Traits]</span>. And as is explained regarding the verse: <span class="verse">"I have wandered like a lost sheep"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 119:176; cf. Likutay Moharan I, 206]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
That is, Yaakov placed all his focus on bringing close the young ones — to extract them from the aspect of Lavan, from the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span> — for they are still tender in years and the reasoning of the errors of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> has not yet taken root in them. But the old ones, who are already rooted in the errors of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, it is difficult to bring them back now to the point of truth — until Heaven has mercy upon them.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And likewise, the entire <span class="key-term">parashah</span> revolves upon this axis — the battle between the point of truth, the aspect of Yaakov, and the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, which is the aspect of Lavan the Aramaean, son of Nachor, as mentioned above. And all the battle and the labor and the toil of Yaakov — everything is for the sake of the children and the sheep, which are the aspect of children and students. For with Yaakov himself, Lavan had no dealings or dispute, as is understood from the <span class="key-term">parashah</span>. For Yaakov himself had already completely shattered the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, the aspect of Lavan, and was completely clean of it, and his hand was withdrawn from it, and he had no dealings with it whatsoever.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
Rather, Yaakov gave up his life with harsh toils and burdens and such stratagems — all for the sake of the sheep and the children. That is, to extract his children and students from the aspect of the house of Lavan, from the aspect of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>. And because the battle is exceedingly great and fierce — for [the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>] strengthens and spreads and extends itself greatly at every level, and the more one vanquishes it a little, the more it extends itself further and further. And as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">"Whoever is greater than his fellow, his [evil] inclination is greater than his"</span> — therefore Yaakov was compelled to flee from him in secret, with Rachel and Leah, who are the generality of the Torah, and with all the children and the sheep.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And Lavan pursued after them and sought to uproot everything, G-d forbid, as it is written: <span class="verse">"The Aramaean sought to destroy my father"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 26:5]</span> — for they had not yet subdued him as was proper. <span class="verse">"Were it not that Hashem was for us"</span>, etc. — in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Were it not that the G-d of my father Avraham and the fear of Yitzchak was for me — you would have sent me away empty. My affliction and the toil of my hands — G-d has seen, and He rebuked [you] last night"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:42]</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
That is, the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Yaakov, says to Lavan that certainly, given the greatness of his pursuit and strengthening against his children and his sheep — certainly it would not have been in my power to stand against you, were it not for the G-d of my father, etc. That is, Hashem, blessed be He, Himself is compelled, as it were, to involve Himself in this, to aid Yisrael who draw close to the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, to save them from all the pursuits of Lavan the Aramaean. As our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">"Were it not for the Holy One, blessed be He, helping him"</span>, etc. — <span class="verse">"as it says: Hashem shall not abandon him in his hand"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 37:33]</span>. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"Were it not that Hashem was my help, my soul would have nearly dwelt in silence"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 94:17]</span>. <span class="precisely">"Silence"</span> (<span class="key-term">dumah</span>) — this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, from which it is impossible to be saved, given the extent of its spread at every level — were it not that Hashem was my help.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is: <span class="verse">"My affliction and the toil of my hands — G-d has seen,"</span> etc. That is, the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span> says that Hashem, blessed be He, has seen his affliction and the toil of his hands. For he himself labored and toiled very, very much for the sake of this flock — to extract them from the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, to elevate them to the intellect, to bring them close to Hashem, blessed be He.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, even though the souls that are drawing close have not yet won the battle as is proper — so that Lavan, the aspect of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, still has the power to pursue them and catch up with them — nevertheless, Hashem, blessed be He, had compassion upon the toils and burdens and self-sacrifice of the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, who gave up his life very much for each and every one. And through this, Hashem, blessed be He, Himself fights with Lavan and drives him away from the sheep of Yaakov, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"My affliction and the toil of my hands — G-d has seen, and He rebuked [you] last night."</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is what Yaakov said to Lavan: <span class="verse">"What is my transgression? What is my sin, that you have pursued after me?"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:36]</span>. That is, Yaakov the true <span class="key-term">tzadik</span> argues with Lavan the Aramaean and says to him: "What is my transgression? What is my sin?" — that is, what sin and transgression have you found in me myself, that you pursue me? You have no power or prosecution against me — for, thank G-d, I am pure and clean and free entirely from all sin and transgression. For I have no gazing at this world at all, not even for the blink of an eye, etc. For <span class="verse">"you have felt about all my vessels — what have you found of all your household goods?"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:37]</span> — for there is in me no hold or connection from you whatsoever. For I am clean of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> completely, completely.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
<span class="verse">"Set it here before my kinsmen and your kinsmen, and let them judge between the two of us"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:37]</span>. That is, [Yaakov] said that he is willing to stand for judgment before whoever [Lavan] wishes — whether before the kinsmen of Yaakov or before the kinsmen of Lavan. That is, whether before those who are on the side of holiness, or before those who are on the other side, on the side of Lavan. For before all of them he would be vindicated in the judgment — for none of them would be able to find in him any blemish or any aspect of hold from any trace of any desire or character trait in the world that is drawn from the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>. For I am clean and pure and free entirely.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
Moreover — I have had great toils and labors in the service of Hashem, blessed be He, for the sake of the totality of Yisrael, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"By day, the heat consumed me, and the frost by night"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:40]</span> — for I labored and toiled greatly in <span class="key-term">hisbodidus</span> <span class="definition">[solitary prayer/meditation]</span> and Torah and prayer, by day and by night, in fields and forests, in heat and great warmth, and in the time of cold and frost at night. For even then, [the <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>] walked on the path alone in <span class="key-term">hisbodidus</span>, and I labored and toiled with very great toils in the service of Hashem — <span class="verse">"and my sleep fled from my eyes"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 31:40]</span> — all for the sake of Hashem, blessed be He, for the sake of bringing close the souls of Yisrael to Him.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
Therefore, even though the souls that are drawing close have not yet won the battle — you have no power to do anything to them. For my power, and the power of my forefathers the true <span class="key-term">tzadikim</span>, will protect them to save them from you. For Hashem, blessed be He, Himself will stand as my aid until I complete with them what I have begun — to bring them all close to Hashem, blessed be He. Until from them will sprout the revelation of the truth to all who come into the world, as it is written: <span class="verse">"Truth shall sprout from the earth"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 85:12]</span>, etc. — from those very ones who are cast down to the earth, from them specifically the truth will sprout to all who come into the world. For <span class="verse">"the speech of truth is established forever"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Proverbs 12:19]</span>, and <span class="verse">"truth endures"</span> <span class="definition">[Aramaic: <span class="key-term">kushta ka'i</span>]</span>.
</p>

<!-- MATNAS SH'CHIV MAYRA -->
<div class="section-num" style="font-size:22px; margin-top:44px;">— Matnas Sh'chiv Mayra —</div>
<div style="text-align:center; font-size:16px; color:#888; margin-bottom:20px;">The Gift of a Dying Person</div>

<!-- SECTION 15 -->
<div class="section-num">— 15 —</div>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-indent:0;">
Let us return to the original matter. And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">matnas sh'chiv mayra</span> <span class="definition">[the gift of a dying person]</span>, which is upheld without a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="talmudic-quote">"The words of a <span class="key-term">sh'chiv mayra</span> are considered as if written and delivered."</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For the essence of all changes and reversals stems from the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span> <span class="definition">[the imaginative/delusional faculty]</span>. For the intellect itself is entirely truth, and there is no change or reversal in it. For the essence of intellect is truth — that is, to grasp and understand every matter to its truth. And when one does not understand through his wisdom and intellect the truth of the matter — even though he articulates many intellectual reasonings regarding this topic — this is not wisdom at all, since he has not arrived at the truth of the matter.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
For example, a doctor: if he proposes many intellectual reasonings that a certain medicine is needed for a certain illness — but in truth this medicine does not help, and on the contrary, it harms — then his wisdom is not considered wisdom at all, since he killed a soul through his wisdom. And regarding such wisdom it is said: <span class="verse">"Your wisdom and your knowledge — it has led you astray"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Isaiah 47:10]</span>. (And see this explained further elsewhere.)
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, the essence of wisdom is that at the beginning of every matter one wishes to do, one should consider with one's intellect the end of the matter and what will grow from this — and not do anything, nor utter any word from his mouth that he will afterward need to retract and change, G-d forbid. And as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">"I never said a thing and retracted."</span> And this is the aspect of: <span class="talmudic-quote">"Who is wise? One who foresees the outcome"</span> — for one must see in every matter that one wishes to speak or do, what will be born from this, so that one will never need to retract and regret it, in this world or in the World to Come.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
It emerges that the essence of wisdom and intellect is truth, and there is no change or reversal in it. And the essence of all changes are in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> that has not been refined — for there all errors and changes are found. For at first it seems to him one way, that he needs to sell this item, and afterward the reasonings of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> are overturned, and he regrets it.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And because of this, our Sages of blessed memory established all the <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> <span class="definition">[formal acts of acquisition]</span> — that the transaction is not completed through speech alone, but rather through a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>. For they assessed through their wisdom that the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> <span class="definition">[knowledge/awareness]</span> is not drawn into the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> except through this <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>. For the essence of all <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> is through the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> (as explained elsewhere). And as long as there has been no <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>, it is possible to change [one's mind] through the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, where all changes reside. For the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> is not drawn into the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span> except through the <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> that our Sages of blessed memory established — each one according to its respective <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, the <span class="key-term">matnas sh'chiv mayra</span>, which takes effect after death, is upheld without a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>. For after death, the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> is nullified — for it is a nullified faculty, and its dominion extends only during one's lifetime, on account of free choice. But after death, the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span> dies and is completely nullified — for there is the essential <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> <span class="definition">[side of death]</span>, and nothing remains of it. For nothing remains of a person after his death except the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> <span class="definition">[the acquired intellect]</span>, which is the true intellect, as explained there at the beginning of the aforementioned Torah-teaching — that the essential enduring aspect of a person after his death is the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, at that time the gift is upheld without a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> at all — through the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> that remains of a person after his death. For the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, from which all changes come, has already been nullified, as mentioned above. For every Jew, even a simple person, has some good point that remains of him, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> — through which he will merit his portion in the World to Come. For <span class="verse">"all Yisrael have a portion in the World to Come."</span>
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is [the meaning of]: <span class="talmudic-quote">"The words of a <span class="key-term">sh'chiv mayra</span> are considered as if written and delivered."</span> For <span class="key-term">writing</span> subdues the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span> — for writing is for remembrance, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Write this as a remembrance in the book"</span> <span class="verse-source">[Exodus 17:14]</span>, etc. And remembrance and forgetting are in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ko'ach hamedameh</span>, as explained elsewhere.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And when one writes a matter as a remembrance, through this one refines the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> — so that forgetting and error and change should not be drawn from it. It emerges that writing is the aspect of the rectification of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mesirah</span> <span class="definition">[delivery/handing over]</span> — that one delivers the item to the domain of the buyer or recipient — which is also the aspect of the subduing of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>. For when the item is delivered and enters from the domain of the giver to the domain of the recipient, then the refinement and ascent of the item are completed — which is the subduing of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">klippos</span> that surround the sparks in the <span class="key-term">masa u'matan</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="paragraph">
And therefore, <span class="talmudic-quote">"the words of a <span class="key-term">sh'chiv mayra</span> are considered as if written and delivered"</span> — for his words are completed and upheld after his death without a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>, as if they were written and delivered — which are the aspect of the subduing of the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>, through which the <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> is completed. For after his death, the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> is nullified, and nothing remains except the aspect of the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> — through which the changes and reversals that come through the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> are nullified, and the <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> is upheld through the aspect of the <span class="key-term">seichel hanikneh</span> that remains of him, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->
<div class="summary-box">
<h3>✦ Translator's Summary ✦</h3>

<p>
<strong>The Core Teaching:</strong> Based on Likutay Moharan I:25 and related Torahs. At every spiritual level, <em>klippos</em> (shells of impurity) surround the holiness. These are subdued through the revelation of the <em>g'vanim</em> (colors) — the greatness of the Creator — which is accessed through <em>tzedakah</em>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Commerce as Spiritual Refinement:</strong> All honest <em>masa u'matan</em> (business dealings) refine holy sparks trapped in the physical world. The refinement is completed when goods pass between domains. The <em>klippos</em> attack precisely at this transition point — hence the need for formal <em>kinyan</em> (acquisition), not mere verbal agreement.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Money, Colors, and the Klippos:</strong> By Torah law, money should suffice for acquisition, because money carries the <em>g'vanim</em> that subdue <em>klippos</em>. But the Sages required <em>meshichah</em> because most people haven't rectified their money through <em>tzedakah</em>, so the colors don't fully illuminate. Without illumination, <em>klippos</em> cling to money — hence the desire for wealth and dishonesty in business.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Gifts are Spiritually Dangerous:</strong> Receiving something for free bypasses the refinement process — the <em>Sitra Achra</em> is itself called <em>chinam</em> (free). Hence "one who hates gifts shall live." The gift deed must say "write it in the marketplace" because the <em>klippos</em> are called <em>chitzonim</em> (outer ones) — publicizing the gift in their territory nullifies their prosecution.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Shabbos — the Ultimate Gift:</strong> Hashem notified us in advance about Shabbos because gifts need preparation. All of Torah and <em>mitzvos</em> are really preparations to receive Hashem's undeserved gifts. This world is "the corridor" and the World to Come is "the banquet hall." Even reward for <em>mitzvos</em> is essentially <em>matnas chinam</em>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Yaakov vs. Lavan — Truth vs. the Imaginative Faculty:</strong> The most extended passage retells the Yaakov/Lavan narrative as the battle between the true <em>tzadik</em> (Yaakov = point of truth) and the <em>ko'ach hamedameh</em> (imaginative/delusional faculty = Lavan). The Torah (Rachel) is deposited in exile within the <em>medameh</em>. The <em>tzadik</em> extracts it, innovates true Torah teachings, and "thrusts the rods" of truth into the minds of his students. The <em>akudim, nekudim, u'vrudim</em> (bound, spotted, speckled) correspond to the three types of intellect and the kabbalistic worlds. The <em>tzadik</em> warms the holy flock against the coldness of Amalek/Lavan, and even though the battle is fierce, Hashem Himself protects those who cleave to the true <em>tzadik</em>. "Truth shall sprout from the earth."
</p>
</div>

<p>
<strong>Matnas Sh'chiv Mayra — The Gift of a Dying Person:</strong> The <em>medameh</em> (imaginative faculty) is the source of all changes and reversals. True intellect is truth — no change. The Sages required <em>kinyan</em> because without it, the <em>medameh</em> causes people to change their minds. But a dying person's gift needs no <em>kinyan</em> — after death, the <em>medameh</em> is completely nullified, leaving only the <em>seichel hanikneh</em> (acquired intellect). Writing = remembrance = rectification of the <em>medameh</em>. Hence "words of a <em>sh'chiv mayra</em> are as if written and delivered."
</p>
</div>

